[Music]  the recent increase in cova cases in  children is leading some parents to  bypass vaccine requirements so kids  younger than 12 can get the shot the fda  recently warned parents not to do this  but the agency may be getting closer to  authorizing the shot for younger kids  pfizer announced this week that its  cobit vaccine is safe and highly  effective in children ages 5 to 11 years  old cbs news chief medical correspondent  dr john lepuc spoke with one parent who  does not want to wait it was probably  the scariest thing as a parent i've had  to deal with lisa shapiro says she felt  helpless watching her daughter suffer  from covid is it going to turn on a dime  and is you know shortness of breath  walking up the stairs going to turn into  serious shortness of breath where she  can't catch her breath while both girls  are okay now shapiro knew she did not  want them to go through it again she got  her 14 year old daughter and payton  vaccinated this summer but her younger  daughter alexandra is only 11. we're  very itching to get it we're impatiently  waiting have you been tempted to ask  your pediatrician  come on she's 11 and a half that's  almost 12. i have been absolutely  tempted i know of some families who have  very large 11 year olds who have sort of  snuck into some of the mass vaccine  sites and things like that dr scott  krugman is vice chair of pediatrics at  the hermann and walter samuelson  children's hospital at sinai in  baltimore  when a parent asks you to vaccinate  their kid who's under 12  what do you say to them it's really  important to wait for the tests to be  done the studies to be done in the  advisory council on immunization  practices and the american kind of  pediatrics to say now's the time and  we're hoping now as the time is coming  sooner rather than later parents are  also hoping authorization comes soon  especially since pfizer said its vaccine  is safe and effective in young children  what do you think about the news coming  out of pfizer about their vaccine for  five to 11 year olds  i'm thrilled i'm excited i've been  waiting for it and hoping for it having  the vaccine is everything it will take  so much worry  away  dr john lepuc is joining us right now  good morning good morning now um should  the authorization process make parents a  little bit more optimistic i think it  should you know nate the fda can't win  right some people say they're moving too  quickly other people saying they're  moving too slowly remember five days ago  there was an advisory meeting of the fda  uh to consider whether the booster  should be given the pfizer booster  should be given for people 16 and over  and you remember the president got a  little bit ahead of this and had raised  expectations that on the 20th of  september that people 16 and above would  would be eligible so when it didn't  happen people were frustrated right i  mean but he did say it has to be  a cdc and fda approved and sure enough  during the advising i sat through eight  hours of them discussing really smart  people and they said you know what  the data's not there yet we're going to  say it's okay for a different group but  we'll see we may change our  recommendation as more information  becomes i think people should find that  reassuring their checks and balances  what are the dangerous potential dangers  i mean what's the big deal basically if  you give a kid 11 year old say an adult  dose of the vaccine well we're always  taught in medical school that kids are  not just little grown-ups so  it's there are changes in hormones kids  are going through puberty uh and they're  very careful about the kind of doses  they give you too much you could get too  much of an inflammatory response you  could get pain at the site fever  headaches joint pains even some rare  inflammatory reactions i spoke to pfizer  yesterday and i talked about the dose  remember 30 micrograms of the physio  dose vaccines given in adults they tried  the 10 20 and 30. they found you know  what 10 the lower dose gave just a  really good response so you always  medicine want to give the lowest amount  of a medicine that still gets the  response well you said you mentioned  that you talked to pfizer yesterday  because now they're saying it's okay for  kids 5 to 11. how soon can we get it  then well it's another example of the  regulatory process that should reassure  people so even though pfizer just came  out this week and said it's safe and  effective the fda says fine we want to  see the data and we think that they're  going to get that visor tells me they're  going to get that to the fda by the end  of this month and then they're going to  not believe what pfizer said they're  going to go through their records and  they're going to do their own statistics  it may take about another month so i'm  hearing maybe around halloween does the  spike in covet cases and children dispel  the narrative that kids they can't get  it yeah  absolutely kids can get it about one in  four of the new cases are in kids you  know we see 28 million kids in this new  age group uh five to 11 so there's a  real opportunity to vaccinate this group  uh we're seeing a a rise in  hospitalization we've done a lot of  stories about that for so long we were  told you don't really have to worry  about kids and parents still lean on  that yeah they do i think that's a false  narrative the general idea is that the  kids are then tend to get less severe  illness but they can still get really  sick and die and remember now as more  and more grown-ups get vaccinated then  it's going to tend to gravitate down to  lower groups that are not vaccinated so  what's your advice on how you can keep  your children safe until they get the  vaccine all right so it's multiple  layers of protection okay so everybody's  got to get vaccinated who can possibly  get vaccine thinking about a school  right everybody should be wearing a mask  who can wear a mask  testing not enough testing's being done  dr rochelle wolensky is the head of the  cdc  was talking with me a couple of weeks  ago and said you know there are billions  of dollars that have been allocated for  testing for schools but the schools  aren't asking for it and finally and  super important is ventilation and  filtration we've been talking about this  trying to hammer this the aerosol  scientists are jumping up and down  saying it's really important it travels  through the air think about cigarette  smoke it's not just six feet or three  feet if you were smoking a cigarette and  we're indoors and it's not well  ventilated am i going to smell it yeah  then potentially you can do that so  merv 13 filters hepa filters open the  doors crack the windows but i think  parents out there should talk to their  school boards and the local authorities  and say what are you doing what's the  plan for ventilation infiltration if the  school isn't testing parents you can  test yourself as well yeah thank you so  much john we appreciate

